 was not in the correct angular mode. He was lucky to get the right answer. The two least positive real values of $x$ for which the sine of $x$ degrees is the same as the sine of $x$ radians are $\\frac{m\\pi}{n-\\pi}$ and $\\frac{p\\pi}{q+\\pi}$, where $m$, $n$, $p$, and $q$ are positive integers. Find $m+n+p+q$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b7d9354607e23a4851e3c45e61b9bd51f990859e809902b1c839792420a00869"},"seconds":66.49302244186401,"seed":199987210280891627,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to find the two least positive real values of \\( x \\) for which the sine of \\( x \\) degrees is the same as the sine of \\( x \\) radians. We start by expressing the sine of \\( x \\) degrees in terms of radians since the sine function is periodic and we can use the periodicity to find our solutions.\n\nThe sine of \\( x \\) degrees is the same as the sine of \\( \\frac{\\pi x}{180} \\) radians. We are given that this is equal to the sine of \\( x \\) radians. Thus, we need to solve:\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{180}\\right) = \\sin(x)\n\\]\n\nThe sine function has the property that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(\\pi - \\theta)\\). Therefore, we have two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = x + 2k\\pi\\) for some integer \\( k \\)\n2. \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = \\pi - x + 2k\\pi\\) for some integer \\( k \\)\n\n### Case 1: \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = x + 2k\\pi\\)\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi x}{180} - x = 2k\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{180} - 1 \\right) = 2k\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\left( \\frac{\\pi - 180}{180} \\right) = 2k\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{2k \\pi \\cdot 180}{\\pi - 180}\n\\]\nFor the smallest positive \\( x \\), we take \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{360\\pi}{\\pi - 180}\n\\]\nThis value is not positive, so we discard this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\(\\frac{\\pi x}{180} = \\pi - x + 2k\\pi\\)\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi x}{180} + x = \\pi + 2k\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{180} + 1 \\right) = \\pi (1 + 2k)\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\left( \\frac{\\pi + 180}{180} \\right) = \\pi (1 + 2k)\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180 \\pi (1 + 2k)}{\\pi + 180}\n\\]\nFor the smallest positive \\( x \\), we take \\( k = 0 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180\\pi}{\\pi + 180} = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi}\n\\]\nFor the next smallest positive \\( x \\), we take \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180\\pi \\cdot 3}{\\pi + 180} = \\frac{540\\pi}{\\pi + 180}\n\\]\n\nSo, the two least positive real values of \\( x \\) are \\( \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi} \\) and \\( \\frac{540\\pi}{180 + \\pi} \\).\n\nRewriting these in the form given in the problem, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi} = \\frac{180\\pi}{180 + \\pi} = \\frac{m\\pi}{n - \\pi} \\quad \\text{with} \\quad m = 180, \\quad n = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{540\\pi}{180 + \\pi} = \\frac{540\\pi}{180 + \\pi} = \\frac{p\\pi}{q + \\pi} \\quad \\text{with} \\quad p = 540, \\quad q = 180\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\( m = 180 \\), \\( n = 180 \\), \\( p = 540 \\), and \\( q = 180 \\). The sum \\( m + n + p + q \\) is:\n\\[\n180 + 180 + 540 + 180 = 1080\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1080}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1080}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
